  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the TNF/TNFR superfamily by linking ligand structure, receptor architecture, and the main cell types each pathway acts on. Across the top, membrane-bound TNF-family ligands are shown as trimeric molecules with extracellular TNF homology domains; several appear to be cleavable ectodomains, indicating that some can also function in soluble form. Arrows connect each ligand to one or more matching receptors below, emphasizing that some interactions are one-to-one while others are shared across multiple receptors.\n\nThe receptors are depicted with characteristic cysteine-rich extracellular domains, and a subset contains intracellular death domains, highlighting a structural division between signaling receptors that promote activation/survival and those capable of death signaling, such as Fas and TRAIL receptors. The diagram also groups receptors associated with immune costimulation, B-cell biology, and tissue remodeling.\n\nThe lower portion maps these receptor systems onto major target cell types. T cells and B cells are prominent targets for many family members, while dendritic cells, fibroblasts, epithelial cells, macrophages, and osteoclasts are affected more selectively. Overall, the image conveys that TNF-family signaling forms a broad communication network controlling immune activation, cell survival or death, and stromal or bone-related responses.",
